      "fields": {
        "Notes": {
          "text": "The Battle of Shanhai Pass on 27th May 1644 was the decisive battle that led to the eventual fall of the Ming Dyansty and the beginning of the Qing dynasty rule in China. The conquest of the entire of China by the Qing took several decades until the supression of the Three Feudatories rebellion in 1683 when the south was finally captured. Epidemic plague ravaged the country in the 1640s which was closely followed by a severe famine. These disasters led to a massive population decline, with some cities such as Hangzhou suffering up to a 50% loss of its people. Peasant rebellions became rife in the 1640s and in 1644 rebels breached the walls of Bejing, leading the emperor to commit suicide. The Little Ice Age is likely to have contributed to the Ming fall as it caused droughts and famine across China in what was the coldest and driest period of 1660-1680. Revolution: unsuccessful revolution involving non-soldiers, supressed by Manchu who then took over",
